WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:04.280
Cruise down the street, and
you leave chaos in your wake.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.960
As you drive or ride
your bike, you've

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.600
probably noticed the
wind whipping around you.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.700
Here's what the
airflow looks like.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.190
These disturbances in
the air and other fluids

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.840
are known as turbulence.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.670
Quantifying these
fragmented flows

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.070
is one of the greatest
problems in math.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:20.990
If researchers can
find a way to capture

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.760
this chaos in an
equation, we'll be

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.580
better able to understand
this universal event,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.620
from locomotion
to ocean currents

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.220
to how gases move
throughout galaxies.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.030
Turbulence is everywhere
if we look close enough.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.320
Blow out a candle and watch the
swirling of the smoke trails.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.240
Disrupt a stream with a stick
and see the little whirlpools

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.590
form in the wake.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.780
On larger scales, the
ocean currents sweep

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.950
across the globe.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.520
Storms churn the atmosphere
on Earth and other planets.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.190
How can researchers study
the chaotic phenomenon

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.190
known as turbulence, and what's
preventing them from capturing

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.420
the full picture?

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.000
Fluids are gases and
liquids that flow and adapt

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.010
to the shape of
a container think

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.380
water, honey, blood, and air.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.000
They have quantifiable
properties

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.340
like viscosity,
velocity, pressure,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.880
and density that mathematicians
can track over time.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.010
They're also governed by
the laws of conservation

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.290
of mass, energy, and momentum.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.680
Mathematicians describe fluid
flows using the Navier-Stokes

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.790
equations.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.190
You can think of these
equations as Newton's second law

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.720
for the motion of fluids.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.390
Force equals mass
times acceleration.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.860
On the left side
of the equation is

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.080
the density and
acceleration, or the change

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.410
in velocity of a particle
of fluid over time.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.390
On the right side is
change in pressure,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.990
internal forces of stress,
and finally, external forces

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.000
acting on the fluid.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.700
Smooth continuous fluid
flows, like in a gentle stream

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.770
or pipe, are called
laminar flows.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:52.650
The equations work well for
describing how particles behave

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.410
in laminar flows, but
most fluids in nature

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:56.860
are not so orderly.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.910
They can be really
chaotic, or turbulent.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.440
In the case of your
afternoon drive,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.450
the air starts as a
laminar flow as it

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.830
meets the front of your car.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.050
What happens next depends
on your car's shape,

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.960
speed, texture, temperature,
as well as the properties

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.760
of the air and atmosphere.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.770
Most cars have an
aerodynamic shape,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.140
which minimizes the
turbulence initially.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.360
As the air moves over
the car, the molecules

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.930
closest to your door stay put
and interact with its surface.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.260
Further away, air molecules
travel at different speeds.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.450
These layers of different air
create friction and turbulence,

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:30.090
what you'd feel as drag.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.040
The result is
vortices and eddies

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.860
that, in turn, generate
more and more swirls.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.840
Ever heard of the
butterfly effect?

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.510
It says a flap of a
butterfly's wing in Japan

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.070
could set off a
chain of events that

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.040
could trigger a tornado
in Texas a week later.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.560
These eddies that beget
more eddies and vortices

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.410
and so on become too
complicated for mathematicians

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.970
to predict or quantify.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.140
Where will eddies form?

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.400
How many will there be?

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.150
Where will energy be
concentrated or dispersed?

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.590
Plug in certain values, and
the equations may spit out

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.720
infinite values for velocity.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.690
This breaks down or
blows up the model.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.180
Because of these challenges
and the possibilities

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.400
that understanding
turbulence could lead to,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.560
there's a million dollar
reward for proving either

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.140
that the Navier-Stokes
equations always work

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.810
or that they sometimes
fail, meaning velocity

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.210
becomes infinite.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.520
Until then, who knows
what chain of events

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.380
you'll start by taking
your Sunday drive.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

